| 2b | AT THE MERGE, ahead of rank 3 | `L47h` the node lend set, light, lend, wear (operator, 2026-11-17; the design at lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/project/light-lend-wear; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/the-worn-house#/the-pulama`>>). ''Built in this span'': the lend core in the mesh (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/lent-faces.ts`, isomorphic by intent, #/rulings); the pūlama's scope, guard and lend frame naming the set (`PulamaScope.faces`, refusing `lent-faces-empty`, `-malformed`, `-duplicate`); the image's admission naming a set at the hand door (`pulama admit [face…]`, its answer naming the set `lent`); the kūkulu gate scoping the set, one vouch and one kūkulu lei per lent face, and an admission naming every lent face's tessera; each session wearing one face inside the lent set at both socket doors (`face-not-lent`, `face-not-named`); the composition naming its own daemon mount (`daemonMountOf`) and the chore rule (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/chore-ask.ts`, `packages/lararium-node/src/chore-face.ts`: `chore-face-not-mounted`); the canon true-up; and the rehearsal on scratch houses (walk 23: three faces lit, the set admitted, one face-scoped read per lent face after the unlock by the pūlama); on the operator's carried house the lit house (rank 4a) lit the three faces, admitted the set `h0`, `h1`, `h2` and unlocked by the pūlama. ''Built since'': the path an operator takes to wear another lent face under the pūlama, as ruled (operator, 2026-11-18, #/rulings: unlock opens custody, `lares wear N` chooses the face inside the lent set and asks no route): `lares wear N` (PR #235), the shims wearing through it (PR #249) and telling the pūlama an unlock's face (PR #314), `rebuild` re-wearing the last face (PR #310), and a vessel Docker restarts wearing the face last worn, the pūlama holding the ordinal in memory alone (PR #312). ''Owed'': one face-scoped read per lent face on the operator's house, unread by this truing | The image the operator stands (rank 3) carries the lend code, and the lit house (rank 4a) admits the set; a re-stand at will that brings back the steward alone would leave the engineer and referee lit in the pūlama and served by no vessel, and the lend serves them all. |

